# G101 — SQL Query Analyzer

*v2. See `sql-query-analyzer.md` for prose: tokenise → parse → analyse
pipeline, AST as plain data, findings with stable codes and severity,
schema-aware validation.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Query Linter

[vault-query-linter @db
    IN ORDER TO
        lint a user's SQL as they type
        surface syntax errors immediately
        and surface semantic findings against the live schema
        without a server round-trip

    @schema <- [db[introspect-schema {connection: @db}]]

    sync @user-events {
        edit @text ->
            @parsed <- [sql[parse {input: @text}]]
                || -> #parse-error @resistance:reason

            @analysis <- [sql[analyse {query: @parsed, schema: @schema}]]

            for @finding in @analysis:findings
                [ui[render-finding {severity: @finding:severity,
                                      code: @finding:code,
                                      message: @finding:message}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[sql
    tokenise        {input}                  ;; || parse-error
    parse           {input}                  ;; || parse-error
    schema
    schema-add      {schema, name, columns}
    analyse         {query, schema}          ;; -> Analysis (never resists)
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: severity ladder as `where` threshold

[sql[analyse {query: @q, schema: @s}]
    where
        severity is one of Info, Warning, Error
        and severity is Info when the finding is an observation
        and severity is Warning when the query is legal but likely wrong
        and severity is Error when the query references something
            that does not exist in the schema
]

[@findings
    where severity at least Warning
    IN ORDER TO
        surface issues the user probably wants to fix
        while allowing Info findings to stay silent by default
]

Findings carry stable codes (`SELECT_STAR`, `LIMIT_WITHOUT_ORDER`,
`UNKNOWN_TABLE`, `FULL_SCAN`) so downstream tooling can suppress or
group by code without parsing the human-readable message.

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: parse and analyse as separate resolver calls

The v1 structure gave one verb that did both. Innate keeps them apart:
the first may resist, the second never does — it only returns findings.

[sql[parse {input: @text}]
    || -> #syntax-error @resistance:reason

@analysis <- [sql[analyse {query: @parsed, schema: @s}]]
@analysis
    where findings count is zero
    || for @f in @analysis:findings -> #semantic-finding @f

---

## Demo

[demo
    @s <- [sql[schema]]
    [sql[schema-add {schema: @s, name: "users",
                      columns: ["id", "name", "email", "created_at"]}]]

    @q1 <- [sql[parse {input: "SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE id = 42"}]]
    -> [sql[analyse {query: @q1, schema: @s}]]
    ;; -> findings: []

    @q2 <- [sql[parse {input: "SELECT * FROM users LIMIT 10"}]]
    -> [sql[analyse {query: @q2, schema: @s}]]
    ;; -> findings: [SELECT_STAR Warning, LIMIT_WITHOUT_ORDER Warning]

    @q3 <- [sql[parse {input: "SELECT birthday FROM widgets"}]]
    -> [sql[analyse {query: @q3, schema: @s}]]
    ;; -> findings: [UNKNOWN_TABLE Error, FULL_SCAN Warning]
]
